<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Brabançonne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Brabançonne is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> of 1830 with words by Jenneval, a French actor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, and music by François Van Campenhout. Its name recalls Brabant, the old duchy at whose heart Brussels lies. The text has been revised several times, and the anthem is sung in French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and German.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-brabanconne.jpg" alt="La Brabançonne" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partition9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title La Brabançonne refers to the Duchy of Brabant. According to legend, the anthem was written in September 1830, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>, by a young revolutionary called Jenneval, who read the lyrics at a meeting in the Aigle d'Or café. Jenneval was a Frenchman, Alexandre Dechet, an actor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> where the revolution began in August 1830; he died in the war of independence.</p><p>François van Campenhout composed the music, based on the tune of a French song, L'Air des lanciers polonais, written by the French poet Eugène de Pradel. That tune was itself an adaptation of L'Air du magistrat irréprochable, found in a popular collection of drinking songs, La Clé du caveau. The anthem was first performed in September 1830.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The original text exists in two versions of 1830. The first, from August, addresses the Belgians as worthy children who should remain under arms, and appeals to Nassau, the House of Orange, to confirm their rights, using the image of the Orange graft that blooms on the tree of Liberty. It warns that if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> are turned on the people, the Orange will fall from the tree. The second version, of September, written after cannon had been fired, declares that grapeshot has shattered the Orange and ends with a lament for the dead.</p><p>The third version dates from 1860, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> formally adopted the song and music as its national anthem. It tells of Belgians who, after centuries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, have reconquered their name, rights and flag, and whose motto is the King, the Law, Liberty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> adopted the anthem in 1860, the prime minister, Charles Rogier, edited out lyrics attacking the Dutch House of Orange, working from the version written by Louis Hymans. A ministerial circular of the Ministry of the Interior on 8 August 1921 decreed that only the fourth verse of Rogier's text should be considered official, in French, German and Dutch. An unofficial short version combining the words of the fourth verse in all three official languages is sung on Belgian National Day, 21 July. A monument of 1930 by the sculptor Charles Samuel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> carries partial lyrics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Brabançonne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brabant's name survives in the Belgian provinces of Flemish Brabant and Walloon Brabant and the Dutch province of North Brabant, the Brussels region lies in the centre of the old duchy, and the Belgian national anthem is called the <b>Brabançonne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
